ty  open for those who fulfil criteria and  even for those who once left it is in  our strategic interest otherwise  somebody else will fill the vacuum  around us at the same time global means  having significant economic power backed  by a strong currency and based on free  but fair international trade the EU has  to remain the champion in this regard  third we need to be honest to our  citizens we cannot promise our citizens  life without problems hard work and  challenges the EU has no a magic land to  solve all problems however we can offer  our citizens a credible vision of our  future and turn that vision into  tangible deeds we need to make our  citizens and above all young people feel  a sense of ownership the EU is not some  fixes distant Brussels we are European  Union's we need to confront  we need to confront those who tell us  otherwise ignoring them or ridiculing  them is not a solution we need to  challenge them by facts the U is now a  large and strong community it provides  us with a collective identity because  there is something about the EU that is  also personal however it does not take  away our national or personal identity  our unity must be a unity of values  within the diversity of our national  regional and local cultures and finally  the fourth we need to care about the  future soon in the run-up of to the  European elections you the European  political parties will have an  opportunity to present a new realistic  vision of our union the european debate  has become very lively and intense at  many levels political and civic national  and regional my appreciations goes to  president McCrone who has significantly  stimulated these discussions with his  own vision of the future and i do agree  with him  we cannot be trapped in the state of quo  and give way to resignation we have to  renew our union it is up to us leaders  to find a common path and instruments  which citizens can trust and support  because we need freedom progress and  protection in the European Union we have  to find a way of protecting the four  freedoms that are at the core of EU  integration we need an EU why debate on  what our strategy and aspirations should  be for the middle of the century the you  will certainly have to undergo a major  transformation towards a climate neutral  economy we need a transition which is  socially just towards our citizens and  fair towards our businesses and  important issue for the future  development of the European economy is  digitalization and automation on the one  hand there is a great concern that  people will not have enough jobs because  robots will replace them on the other  hand they are great concerns about the  lack of skilled workers on the market  yet in the future we shall need even  more highly skilled people the best  evidence of this is that the countries  with the highest number of robots per  job as Germany South Korea Singapore  have the lowest unemployment rate they  are currently around 151 robots per  10,000 working places in Slovakia that  is more than half the European average  and the highest level in Central and  Eastern Europe and we should not be  afraid of technological change we only  need to adapt smart and responsible  policies if you want to be honest in our  aspiration for the future we must have  the right tools such as the next  multi-annual financial framework the  current EU budget represents only 1% of  EU GNA  focusing the budget on new challenges we  should not give up on cohesion policy  calls for its reduction in the next mff  reflect of wrong understanding of the  role of cohesion policy it is indeed  cohesion policy that helps rich and poor  regions to converge and contributes to  economic growth even in difficult  economies times I mentioned I mentioned  that we need to speak about our future  and please how can we speak about the  new mff without to have a clear vision  of the European Union  I don't think that it is a right  approach that first we see  which kind of budget we have and then we  decide what we can pay out of this  budget I think we should do it otherwise  first we have to see where we would like  to be in next 5 7 10 15 years which are  our priorities and then to decide how  big and which kind of budget for our  priorities we need this is the approach  which I like more than than what we do  in this moment and when I speak about  new technologies please we have to say  very open that and I'm not speaking  about European Parliament but in general  at the European level we are not able to  catch the speed of the development in  this area we are always behind we are  facing a digital revolution that we are  not able to do some revolution in the in  the regulatory framework we are always  behind we are trying to regulate  something which already people are using  and sometimes we are doing such a  regulation that we over regulate  everything and then we are crying why we  are not at the same level than United  States or Asia where the innovation  environment is growing up and we are  still divided in 27 or 28 28 different  regulations so please we need to think  about some flexible legal regulatory  framework not to regulate each simple  thing which is going on in our lives  because this is not the future in the  digital revolution we need to put only  some basic rules on the table and some  framework which is quite flexible enough  because we politicians are not able to  act as fast as we need to act and that's  why I mentioned the the world if we  speak about industry 4-0 we need to push  our member state that in their stage we  have also government's 4-0 because in  this moment I think we have in some  countries maybe government 2-0 somewhere  maybe 3-0 but I'm not sure if we have  somewhere government's 4-0 which are  reflecting the needs of the  ladies and gentlemen the European  elections are quickly approaching and we  need to mobilize our forces to fight  against fake news hybrid threats and  cyber propaganda make people feel the  urgency talk to them about the  importance of the U for their everyday  life make them understand that elections  matter that is their only opportunity to  steer the U and they should not let  other speaks for them there is a lot of  work ahead of us and let me finish my  speech with the words of one of the  founding fathers of European integration  Jean Monnet people only accept change  when they are faced with necessity and  only recognize necessity when a crisis  is upon them we must be brave and  determined we have to make changes and  make the union attractive to all a union  that is able able to learn from its past  mistakes in order to prepare itself to  embrace the future and once again  we need a vision of an attractive union  that citizens can trust and support  thank you for your attention  thank you very much mr. prime minister  for your speech the next speaker is the  president the European Commission  jean-claude you care bonjourno mr.  president mr. prime minister dear Peter  confianza clay well allow me to tell you  a secret I didn't sleep much because if  mrs. main last night but I slept enough  time to dream of Slovakia  because negotiating with mrs. main  yesterday I thought about the September  2016 Bratislava summit three months  after the decision of the British people  to leave the European Union your  government one of which were you apart  and you have deserved a great deal of  merit as a result of the Slovakian  presidency for the first time Slovakia  hosted a summit of the 27 EU members  because it felt that brexit belonged to  the past and it was time to prepare the  future of the European Union that's what  we did it brother Slava by adopting the  outcome document of Bratislava that  meeting took place a few days after the  State of the Union address I gave him in  the parliament and for the first perhaps  the last time the 27 prime ministers  welcomed the content of that speech it  had never happened before  and it will probably never happen again  but I will remember it as a high point  of my small political career the  Bratislava summit showed that with  courage and with determination we can  move towards a European future which  some believed would never be possible  after the British decision last night  we've reached an agreement with the  British government about the conditions  and consequences of brexit and what I  said last night that it was a second  chance but there wouldn't be a third so  I hope very much that the members of the  Parliament of the UK today support the  approach that has been adopted from it  and adopt the agreed text representing  the agreement that has been reached  Peter allow me to thank you on behalf of  the Commission for all of the support  that your government has provided and  continues to provide I'm particularly  thinking of the effort that has been  made towards innovation on the directive  for the posting of workers and the same  applies to the labor Authority I would  note also that governments that want to  host that authority on their and  territory are more numerous than those  who initially supported the creation of  such an authority  I'm delighted to hear you say the new  like the social pillar of the Univ the  European Union the more Prime Minister's  who say that not only here not just at  the Parliament but also at the council  well the less isolated I will feel  within the European Union your  government has helped us to put the  social pillar into place the special  summit which took place in good åberg  there is a need to establish social  standards in Europe that many people are  talking about the need to introduce a  europe-wide minimum wage this is not a  new idea it's something that has been  suggested since the beginning of this  commission the same applies to the  creation of a minimum revenue in all  European countries you talked about the  seven-year financial framework for the  for this budget and we need to establish  an agreed budget this is exactly what  the Commission has been working towards  that was our approach and it certainly  corresponds with the wish that you have  expressed he also said we must not  ignore the need to keep the cohesion  policy in place and I am a fervent  supporter of the cohesion policy but if  we agree on policy then as you have  yourself called for we also need not to  forget policies of innovation  modernization promoting digital economy  in defence if we were not able to review  the cohesion policy which is still a  necessity as well as the common a group  Common Agricultural Policy  we would not have been able to achieve  what we have even your government as  enthusiastic as you are would never have  accepted a budget with 141 percent total  compared to the last one so we have  proceeded according to that need  Slovakia has the presidency of the visa  grant group at the moment and I would  like you to take this opportunity today  to try to put an end to this fruitless  debate with the east of Europe and  Central Europe Europe needs to breathe  with two lungs and we need to ensure  that they are not contradicting one  another so that they can work  effectively together we all have a  contribution to make it is not just a  Commission and the European Parliament  and a few European governments of  Western Europe that are responsible for  this confrontation which is sometimes  ridiculous and certainly harmful so I  urge you to use your presidency of the  Rizk red group to remedy this situation  as you know Slovakia for us from the  west of Europe and who are relatively  young or at least who try to stay young  Slovak here for us is not a neutral  country  I was 13 years old at the time of Prague  Spring time which was also the time for  Bratislava Spring I indeed the first  movements towards that was seen in  Slovakia so we opener attention to this  country the particular affection born of  experience and hope so I hope very much  that the Slovaks do not think that the  rest of Europe does not like Slovakia  the opposite is true and I would like to  see Slovakia feel more affection towards  the European Union as well there are  historical events which are little known  the exile government of Czechoslovakia  at the time outlined a project for  Europe which they called Project for a  European Union and in this little-known  document a single phrase can be found  which is always touched me these  countries of Central Europe have wrote  the last is the last sentence we would  like to see the countries of Western  Europe join us to build the European  Union that was the government in exile  that wrote that and history prevented  them subsequently from bringing that  vision to be given that it was Western  Europe that enjoyed peace initially I  think we all have the obligation to  continue working towards the work and  the objectives we set ourselves thank  you now see Rhoda Nathan Arbuckle Marcia  thank you Joe Claude  we will now begin with speakers on  behalf political groups responds first  over to you thank you mister Janka for  your kind words thank you to the Prime  Minister a warm welcome to the
 

